 "erddap_ostia": {
  "url": "https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/search/index.csv?page=1&itemsPerPage=40&searchFor=OSTIA",
  "status": 200,
  "head": "griddap,Subset,tabledap,Make A Graph,wms,files,Accessible,Title,Summary,FGDC,ISO 19115,Info,Background Info,RSS,Email,Institution,Dataset ID\nhttps://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/ecocast,,,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/ecocast.graph,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/wms/ecocast/request,,public,\"Relative Bycatch:Target Catch Probability Product (daily), EcoCast Project \",\"The Relative Bycatch:Target Catch Probability Product is produced using a data-driven, multi-species predictive habitat modelling framework. First, boosted regression tree models were fit to determine the habitat preferences of the target species, broadbill swordfish (Xiphias gladius), and three bycatch-sensitive species that interact with the California drift gillnet fishery (leatherback sea turtle (Dermochelys coricea), blue shark (Prionace glauca), California sea lion (Zalophus californianus)). Then, individual species weightings were set to reflect the level of bycatch and management concern for each species. Prediction layers for each species were then combined into a single surface by multiplying the layer by the species weighting, summing the layers, and then re-calculating the range of values in the final predictive surface from -1 (low catch & high bycatch probabilities) to 1 (high catch & low bycatch probabilities).\\n\\ncdm_data_type = Grid\\nVARIABLES (all of which use the dimensions [time][latitude][longitude]):\\necocast (EcoCast Relative Bycatch-Target Catch Probability Product, 1)\\n\",https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/metadata/fgdc/xml/ecocast_fgdc.xml,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/metadata/iso19115/xml/ecocast_iso19115.xml,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/info/ecocast/index.csv,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/ecocast,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/rss/ecocast.rss,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/subscriptions/add.html?datasetID=ecocast&showErrors=false&email=,NOAA NMFS SWFSC ERD,ecocast\nhttps://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/ecocast_Lon0360,,,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/ecocast_Lon0360.graph,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/wms/ecocast_Lon0360/request,,public,\"Relative Bycatch:Target Catch Probability Product (daily), EcoCast Project, Lon0360\",\"The Relative Bycatch:Target Catch Probability Product is produced using a data-driven, multi-species predictive habitat modelling framework. First, boosted regression tree models were fit to determine the habitat preferences of the target species, broadbill swordfish (Xiphias gladius), and three bycatch-sensitive species that interact with the California drift gillnet fishery (leatherback sea turtle (Dermochelys coricea), blue shark (Prionace glauca), California sea lion (Zalophus californianus)). Then, individual species weightings were set to reflect the level of bycatch and management concern for each species. Prediction layers for each species were then combined into a single surface by multiplying the layer by the species weighting, summing the layers, and then re-calculating the range of values in the final predictive surface from -1 (low catch & high bycatch probabilities) to 1 (high catch & low bycatch probabilities).\\n\\ncdm_data_type = Grid\\nVARIABLES (all of which use the dimensions [time][latitude][longitude]):\\necocast (EcoCast Relative Bycatch-Target Catch Probability Product, 1)\\n\",https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/metadata/fgdc/xml/ecocast_Lon0360_fgdc.xml,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/metadata/iso19115/xml/ecocast_Lon0360_iso19115.xml,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/info/ecocast_Lon0360/index.csv,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/ecocast,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/rss/ecocast_Lon0360.rss,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/subscriptions/add.html?datasetID=ecocast_Lon0360&showErrors=false&email=,NOAA NMFS SWFSC ERD,ecocast_Lon0360\n,,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/tabledap/ecocast_inputs,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/tabledap/ecocast_inputs.graph,,,public,Ecocast species weightings and environmental data dates,\"The dataset contains the species weightings and the dates of the environmental data used for each EcoCast Map dataset (https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/ecocast)\\n\\ncdm_data_type = Grid\\nVARIABLES:\\ntime (Centered Time, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\ndate\\nblueshark (Blue Shark Bycatch Model Weighting)\\nbluesharkTRK (Blue Shark Tracking Model Weighting)\\nsealions (California Sea Lions Tracking Model Weighting)\\nleatherbacks (Leatherback Turtles Model Weighting)\\nswordfish (Swordfish Model Weighting)\\nsst_time (Date of Sea Surface Temperature Data, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\nchl_time (Date of Chlorophyll-a Data, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\neke_time (Date of Eddy Kinetic Energy Data, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\nssh_time (Date of Sea Surface Height Data, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\nwind_time (Date of Wind Data, seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z)\\n\",,,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/info/ecocast_inputs/index.csv,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/ecocast,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/rss/ecocast_inputs.rss,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/subscriptions/add.html?datasetID=ecocast_inputs&showErrors=false&email=,NOAA NMFS SWFSC ERD,ecocast_inputs\nhttps://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/nesdisBLENDEDsstDLDaily,,,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/nesdisBLENDEDsstDLDaily.graph,https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/wms/nesdisBLENDEDsstDLDaily/request,,public,\"Sea-Surface Temperature, NOAA Geo-polar Blended Analysis Diurnal Correction (Day+Night), GHRSST, Near Real-Time, Global 5km, 2019-Present, Daily\",\"Analysed blended sea surface temperature over the global ocean using day and night input data. An SST estimation scheme which combines multi-satellite retrievals of sea surface temperature datasets available from polar orbiters, geostationary InfraRed (IR) and microwave sensors into a single"
 },
